edge device: 2048 MB RAM, no swap, 1 MB modelled pages
three tasks need 64 + 1500 + 128 = 1692 MB (fits)

A. plain LRU, three tasks
  control (RT)   working set    64 MB   faults      64
  ML inference   working set  1500 MB   faults    1500
  logger         working set   128 MB   faults     128
  total                            1692

B. control task pinned, three tasks
  control (RT)   working set    64 MB   faults      64
  ML inference   working set  1500 MB   faults    1500
  logger         working set   128 MB   faults     128
  total                            1692

C. plain LRU, fourth 500 MB task added (2192 MB > 2048 MB)
  control (RT)   working set    64 MB   faults      64
  ML inference   working set  1500 MB   faults    2846
  logger         working set   128 MB   faults     291
  4th task       working set   500 MB   faults     679
  total                            3880

D. control task pinned, fourth task added
  control (RT)   working set    64 MB   faults      64
  ML inference   working set  1500 MB   faults    2846
  logger         working set   128 MB   faults     291
  4th task       working set   500 MB   faults     679
  total                            3880

interpretation:
  A/B: the three working sets fit, so no capacity misses and
       pinning changes latency only, not the fault count.
  C:   1692 + 500 = 2192 MB exceeds RAM, so LRU starts evicting
       ML pages and the fault count jumps from 1692 to 3880.
  D:   the fault counts are the same as C, and the control task
       still takes only 64 faults: its pages are re-referenced
       every cycle, so LRU keeps them resident even unpinned.
       Pinning makes that a guarantee rather than a likelihood;
       it does not reduce a fault count that is already zero.
